from lesson_writer import author,CPP

def w(name,purpose,steps,adapt,tests,exercise,limits):
 author('src/org/minima/objects/'+name+'.md',purpose,steps,adapt,tests,exercise,limits+' Source-map status is partial and parity not_verified.')
def obj(detail):
 return lambda l: detail+' See '+l(CPP/'support/ObjectOracle.java','Java object oracle')+', '+l(CPP/'support/objects.cpp','native object harness')+' and '+l(CPP/'tools/object_differential.py','object driver')+'. The inspected existing full-object report has 9,244 successful comparisons; this is a corpus total, not a count of this file’s tests. Generic ERROR normalization leaves exact exception types unproven.'
w('Pulse','A compact synchronization message containing a version and a list of recent block identifiers.',[
 'Initialize an empty block list. setBlockList replaces the Java list reference, and getBlockList exposes that mutable list.',
 'Write mutable PULSE_VERSION, then a MiniNumber count, then each block ID using ordinary MiniData encoding. The unused mPulsePoW field contributes no wire bytes.',
 'On read, replace the block list before decoding the version. Compare the incoming version with literal ONE, rather than the mutable PULSE_VERSION field.',
 'For an unknown version, log and return immediately with an empty list. The rest of the input remains unread. For version one, decode the count and append each ID.',
 'The Java createPulse factory holds the database read lock while obtaining TxPowTree.getPulseList, logs caught exceptions, unlocks and returns the assembled pulse.'
], 'Native vectors replace Java lists by value. The matching serialization and static decoder exist, but the inspected native header does not expose createPulse or the unused proof-of-work field. Returning a new decoded object also does not reproduce all mutation observations of reading into an existing Java instance.',
 obj('The pulse branch creates lists of 0, 1, 3, 60, 61 and 128 IDs and compares bytes and returned IDs. Explicit version 0, 1 and 2 payloads test accepted versus early-return paths, including remaining-byte count. Java-produced round trips plus truncation/trailing variants exercise decoding. These fixtures do not call createPulse or establish actual database-lock behavior.'),
 'If PULSE_VERSION is changed to 2 before writing, will the same class’s reader accept that message? Explain using the exact comparison in readDataStream.',
 'Creating a live pulse from the current chain remains an API/runtime gap. Version-mutation fixtures observe returned data and remaining bytes; they do not compare the diagnostic log text.')
w('TxBody','The transaction-bearing part of a TxPoW, containing normal and burn transactions, their witnesses, randomness, difficulty and referenced TxPoW IDs.',[
 'Initialize a fresh 32-byte random PRNG field and maximal transaction difficulty. Create independent empty normal/burn Transaction and Witness objects and an empty referenced-ID list.',
 'resetRandomPRNG replaces only the random bytes. It does not update a containing TxPoW’s cached hashes automatically.',
 'JSON emits prng, txndiff, txn, witness, burntxn, burnwitness and txnlist. List order is retained.',
 'Serialize PRNG with hash-length limits, transaction difficulty with ordinary MiniData encoding, the two transaction/witness pairs, then a MiniNumber list count and hash-encoded referenced IDs. The two MiniData encoding choices have distinct size validation.',
 'Java reading replaces PRNG and difficulty but calls readDataStream on the already existing transaction and witness objects. It replaces the referenced-ID list only after those nested reads complete, so failure ordering and retained references are observable.'
], 'The native body stores all fields by value and its static deserializer constructs fresh nested objects. Its member initializers request randomness even when deserialization will immediately overwrite PRNG. This composition reuses Transaction and Witness serializers, but it does not yet provide a matching mutating body read API.',
 obj('The body fixture fixes PRNG bytes so random initialization does not make comparisons nondeterministic, then compares full JSON and bytes. Body-containing TxPoW and TxBlock fixtures exercise nested composition, and generated read cases test incomplete/trailing payloads. Deterministic fixtures do not establish random-source quality or retained nested-reference parity.'),
 'Why must a test replace PRNG before comparing independently constructed Java and C++ bodies? Which hash in a containing TxPoW becomes stale if PRNG changes afterward?',
 'The file is a data container, not a proof-of-work or transaction verifier. Java reference identity across readDataStream and partial mutation need tests beyond fresh-object round trips.')
def greettests(l):
 return 'The object fixture varies top block, chain length and welcome text, comparing wire bytes, root height and extra JSON. More directly, '+l(CPP/'support/NetworkStartupOracle.java','NetworkStartupOracle')+' and '+l(CPP/'support/network_startup.cpp','native startup harness')+' invoke createGreeting against controlled database/user/peer services. They start with retained JSON and a preexisting chain entry, repeat calls, and record call order, lock depth, appended IDs, fields and bytes. The '+l(CPP/'tools/network_startup_differential.py','startup driver')+' injects faults at lock, unlock, welcome, tip and peers; varies missing/empty/invalid peers and host/P2P flags; and includes 120 seeded sequences of 12 calls. This is a controlled service fixture, not a complete running node. Generic exceptions are normalized. The inspected existing startup report records 532 matching cases, including 1,764 createGreeting calls, across greeting and connect-command work.'
w('Greeting','The initial peer message describing node version, extra connection metadata and the sender’s chain from tip toward root.',[
 'Initialize version from GlobalParams, empty extra JSON, top block zero and an empty chain. createGreeting mutates and returns the same object.',
 'Acquire the database read lock before entering the try block. Add welcome, optional configured host and port, then inspect the chain tip. A missing tip sets top block to -1.',
 'Walk tip to parent repeatedly and append each TxPoW ID. The method does not clear existing IDs first; calling it twice accumulates chains. Likewise omitted host or peer fields can retain prior JSON values.',
 'If P2P is enabled, obtain the peer list and serialize addresses into extra JSON. Catch and log exceptions from inside the body, then unlock afterward. Lock/unlock calls themselves sit outside that catch.',
 'getRootBlock returns -1 for top -1 or top above one trillion. Otherwise it computes top - (chain size - 1); an empty chain therefore gives top + 1 rather than being separately rejected.',
 'Write version, extra JSON as MiniString text, top block, count and ordinary MiniData IDs. Reading parses the extra JSON: ParseException becomes a logged empty object, while a successfully parsed wrong type has different cast behavior.'
], 'Native GreetingServices injects the database lock, welcome, tip and peers operations. It reproduces the ordering and repeated append behavior using the mapped tree nodes and address serializer. Missing callbacks fail on use; this boundary must be connected by the runtime. Optional JSON can represent parsed null, and getExtraData then fails, corresponding only approximately to Java null dereference behavior.',
 greettests,
 'After two createGreeting calls on a chain of three nodes, starting with one retained ID, how many IDs are present? Explain why a later failure at peers can still leave the new top block and appended chain visible.',
 'The service harness deliberately bypasses full Java Main startup. Runtime integration, exact logging and all JSON cast/null exceptions remain separate verification work. The native getRootBlock also omits Java’s extra logging around a NumberFormatException during subtraction.')
